Human Resilience and Space-Readiness Blueprint (HRSB)

An Evidence-Based Systems Architecture for Healthspan, Physiological Resilience, and Human Performance

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Gemini 

Version: v0.4

Research Paradigm: Systems Engineering / Preventive Medicine / Human Physiology / Space Medicine / Healthy Aging / Computational Health



Abstract

Version 0.4 of the Human Resilience and Space-Readiness Blueprint (HRSB) builds upon the structural foundation established in v0.3—the separation of a long-term research vision (Layer A) from a tractable dissertation core (Layer B)—while incorporating five operational refinements that address previously identified mathematical, methodological, and evidentiary vulnerabilities. The core theoretical contribution of this version is the transition from additive linear time-invariant (LTI) dynamics to a bilinear control-affine state-space formulation, which correctly represents the multiplicative nature of physiological countermeasure interventions on deconditioning rate constants rather than their additive effect on state variables. The deterministic viability kernel of v0.3 is replaced by a stochastic viability formulation grounded in the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) partial differential equation, yielding a probabilistic viability map that accounts for intrinsic biological noise and measurement uncertainty. The Transferability Matrix \mathbf{T}, previously a conceptual taxonomy, is now given an explicit gradient-based estimator that permits empirical calculation from model parameters estimated on bed-rest datasets. An asynchronous observation model with indicator-matrix projection is specified to accommodate the heterogeneous sampling frequencies characteristic of NASA Open Science Data Repository (OSDR) head-down-tilt bed rest studies. Finally, each core research hypothesis is anchored to an explicit statistical falsification criterion, ensuring Popperian testability and defense readiness.

This version explicitly acknowledges that no empirical work, physical testing, or computational training has been conducted to date. The document therefore provides a complete implementation roadmap—spanning data acquisition protocols, model estimation algorithms, numerical methods for viability computation, and publication targets—that constitutes the executable research program for the doctoral dissertation. The framework remains committed to the principle that the physiological requirements of long-duration spaceflight, when properly characterized as an extreme reference environment, can illuminate transferable principles of human resilience applicable to terrestrial healthspan optimization, while respecting the fundamental asymmetries and antagonisms that distinguish Earth-based optimization from space-readiness.

Keywords: systems physiology, bilinear control systems, stochastic viability theory, Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equations, state-space modeling, transferability matrix, head-down-tilt bed rest, healthspan optimization, space medicine, evidence governance

