2. Layer A and Layer B: The Two-Layer Architecture

2.1 Rationale for the Two-Layer Structure

The HRSB maintains the two-layer architecture established in v0.3:

Layer A — The Vision (Long-Term Research Program): Preserves the nine-domain architecture (cardiovascular, musculoskeletal, metabolic, neuro-endocrine, immune, cellular, sleep-circadian, nutritional, psychological), the Earth–Space transferability taxonomy, the full state-space specification, and the PINDE computational architecture as a multi-institute, multi-decade research roadmap.

Layer B — The Dissertation Core (Scoped, Testable, Falsifiable): Fully operationalizes the cardiovascular and musculoskeletal domains through named variables, a specified bilinear control-affine model class, empirical validation against NASA bed-rest data, and a numerical stochastic viability computation.

This restructuring does not abandon the ambition of the original framework. It sequences the work: the dissertation demonstrates, on a tractable subset, that the systems-engineering approach yields concrete, publishable results; the Layer A architecture then extends the validated methodology to the remaining domains.

2.2 Prior Art and Explicit Differentiation

The concept of a physiological digital twin is not novel. The Astronaut Digital Twin (ADT) describes a platform designed to mirror astronaut physiology, apply countermeasures, and predict system response. Domain-specific astronaut digital twins have been published, including a feasibility study on orthopedic bone-loss modeling under artificial-gravity and countermeasure regimes. At the terrestrial-health end, the European Union has funded EDITH, a coordination-and-support action explicitly building toward a "Virtual Human Twin."

The HRSB differs from these initiatives in three specific ways:

First, the HRSB explicitly couples the terrestrial-healthspan objective to the space-readiness objective as two modes of the same optimization problem. Neither ADT (space-only) nor EDITH (Earth-only, disease-focused) currently does this.

Second, the HRSB adopts stochastic viability theory as the formal optimization framework, shifting the objective from point-optimization to maximizing the probability-weighted volume of the viability kernel. This contrasts with the trajectory-prediction emphasis of existing digital twin work.

Third, the HRSB enforces a rigorous evidence hierarchy (GRADE) that separates established interventions from experimental longevity claims—a governance mechanism absent from the engineering-focused ADT and disease-focused EDITH frameworks.

A note on terminology: "resilience engineering" is an established term of art in safety-critical systems engineering, referring to how organizations absorb and adapt to unexpected disruptions. The HRSB uses "resilience" in the physiological-reserve sense—the capacity of biological systems to maintain function under stress—and this distinction should be kept explicit to avoid terminological confusion across disciplines.

