3. The Human Being as a Dynamic System (Layer A Formalization)

3.1 Continuous-Time Stochastic Dynamics

The full nine-domain system is formalized as a continuous-time stochastic differential equation (SDE):

dX(t) = f\big(X(t), U(t), E(t), \theta(t)\big)\,dt + \mathbf{G}\big(X(t)\big)\,dW(t)

where:

· X(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n is the true biological state vector across cardiovascular, metabolic, musculoskeletal, neuro-endocrine, and cellular domains;
· U(t) \in \mathcal{U} \subset \mathbb{R}^m represents behavioral and therapeutic interventions (exercise, nutrition, sleep scheduling, pharmacological agents);
· E(t) \in \mathcal{E} \subset \mathbb{R}^q represents environmental conditions (microgravity, radiation, light-dark cycles, thermal stress, confinement);
· \theta(t) \in \mathbb{R}^p represents time-varying, subject-specific biological parameters (e.g., insulin sensitivity, vascular compliance, bone turnover rate, circadian phase);
· \mathbf{G}(X(t)) \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n} is the state-dependent noise diffusion matrix;
· W(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n is a standard Wiener process capturing intrinsic biological stochasticity.

3.2 The State Vector Decomposition

X(t) = \begin{bmatrix}
X_{\text{cardio}}(t) \\
X_{\text{musculo}}(t) \\
X_{\text{metab}}(t) \\
X_{\text{neuro}}(t) \\
X_{\text{immuno}}(t) \\
X_{\text{cellular}}(t)
\end{bmatrix}

Each component is itself a vector. The full state space dimension n may exceed 50–100 variables when fully specified. This full specification is retained as the Layer A long-term objective, but the dissertation core (Layer B, Section 6) commits to a reduced 4-variable model with explicit functional forms.

3.3 Partial Observability

Because true biological state X(t) cannot be continuously measured in its entirety, the framework operates under partial observability:

Y(t) = g\big(X(t)\big) + \eta(t)

where Y(t) \in \mathbb{R}^k represents streaming sensor measurements, intermittent clinical biomarkers, and self-reported metrics; g: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^k is the observation mapping; and \eta(t) \in \mathbb{R}^k is sensor noise, assumed zero-mean Gaussian with covariance \mathbf{R}(t).

3.4 Subsystem Coupling

The central strength of a systems framework lies in nonlinear cross-domain interactions. The interaction matrix \mathbf{J} = \partial f / \partial X captures how perturbations in one physiological domain propagate to others. Key coupling mechanisms include:

· Microgravity / Fluid Shift → Autonomic & Sleep: Cephalad fluid shift increases central venous pressure, altering baroreflex sensitivity and sleep-arousal thresholds.
· Circadian Disruption → Metabolic Flexibility: Altered PER/CRY gene expression disrupts peripheral clock alignment in skeletal muscle, impairing glucose disposal.
· Musculoskeletal Loading → Neuro-Cognitive Reserve: Myokine secretion (irisin, BDNF) links mechanical resistance training to hippocampal neuroplasticity.
· Sleep Fragmentation → Immune & Inflammatory State: Reduced slow-wave sleep impairs glymphatic clearance and increases IL-6, TNF-α.
· Nutritional Deficiency → Musculoskeletal Recovery: Inadequate protein intake impairs mTOR-mediated muscle protein synthesis post-exercise.

This coupling matrix is not merely descriptive—it defines the structure of the dynamical system and constrains the types of control strategies that can be effective. However, for the Layer B dissertation core, we deliberately restrict to the cardiovascular–musculoskeletal coupling, which is the most directly measurable and the most directly stressed by microgravity analogs.

3.5 Time-Scale Separation

Physiological subsystems operate on vastly different time scales:

· Fast dynamics (seconds–minutes): cardiovascular regulation, autonomic tone, acute glucose response
· Medium dynamics (hours–days): sleep-wake cycle, glycogen stores, fluid balance
· Slow dynamics (weeks–months): muscle hypertrophy, bone remodeling, metabolic adaptation
· Very slow dynamics (years–decades): aging processes, degenerative changes

This suggests a singular perturbation structure:

\frac{dX}{dt} = f_{\text{fast}}(X_{\text{fast}}, X_{\text{slow}}, U, E) + \varepsilon f_{\text{slow}}(X_{\text{fast}}, X_{\text{slow}}, U, E)

where \varepsilon \ll 1. For the Layer B core, we focus on the weeks-to-months timescale of musculoskeletal and cardiovascular deconditioning under bed rest, treating faster dynamics as quasi-static.

