7. Stochastic Viability Theory

7.1 From Deterministic to Stochastic Viability

Version 0.3 invoked viability theory (Aubin, 1991) with a deterministic constraint set K and proposed Saint-Pierre's viability kernel algorithm. Critical review identified a fundamental limitation: biological systems are inherently stochastic, with process noise \mathbf{w}(t) \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{Q}) that renders deterministic boundaries unviable. A momentary dip below x_1 = 25 mL/kg/min due to measurement noise or biological fluctuation would cause the deterministic algorithm to falsely classify a subject as non-viable.

Version 0.4 therefore replaces the deterministic viability kernel with a stochastic viability formulation grounded in the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) framework.

7.2 The Stochastic Viability Value Function

Definition 7.1 (Stochastic Viability Value Function). Given the bilinear dynamical system (Section 6.3), the safe physiological envelope K, and time horizon T, the stochastic viability value function v(x, t) is defined as:

v(x, t) = \sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \mathbb{E} \left[ \inf_{s \in [t, T]} \mathbf{1}_{K}\big(X(s)\big) \;\middle\vert\; X(t) = x \right]

The value v(x, t) \in [0, 1] represents the maximal probability that the biological system remains within the safe physiological envelope K for all future times s \in [t, T], given that the system is in state x at time t and optimal control u is applied.

Interpretation:

· v(x, t) \to 1: High-confidence viable region. The system can be maintained within safe limits with near-certainty.
· v(x, t) \in (0.5, 1): Marginal viability zone. There exists a non-trivial probability of constraint violation.
· v(x, t) \in (0, 0.5): Unviable region. Even optimal control cannot guarantee safe operation.
· v(x, t) = 0: Completely unviable. No control strategy can maintain the system within K.

7.3 The Constraint Set as a Signed Distance Function

To enable smooth numerical computation, the indicator function \mathbf{1}_K(x) is replaced by a signed distance function \ell(x) that encodes the constraint set continuously:

\ell(x) = \min \left\{ x_1 - 25, \;\; 80 - x_2, \;\; x_3 - 0.7, \;\; x_4 - 1.0 \right\}

The safe set is then characterized by \ell(x) \ge 0, with the boundary at \ell(x) = 0. The signed distance formulation allows the terminal condition of the HJB equation to be expressed as a smooth function:

v(x, T) = \mathbf{1}_{\{x \mid \ell(x) \ge 0\}}(x)

7.4 The Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman Equation

The dynamic programming equation governing the time evolution of the viability probability value function v(x, t) is:

-\frac{\partial v}{\partial t} - \max_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left\{ \left( \mathbf{A}_0 x + \sum_{j=1}^{m} u_j \mathbf{A}_j x + \mathbf{C} E \right)^T \nabla_x v + \frac{1}{2} \text{Tr}\left( \mathbf{Q} \, \nabla_x^2 v \right) \right\} = 0

with terminal condition v(x, T) = \mathbf{1}_{\{x \mid \ell(x) \ge 0\}}(x).

Numerical solution: This equation is a second-order nonlinear PDE that can be solved using standard level-set methods (e.g., helperOC toolbox, optimized_dp toolbox). The computational cost scales as O(N^4) for a 4-variable system with N grid points per dimension, requiring adaptive grid refinement or GPU acceleration for fine resolution.

7.5 Stochastic Viability Map

The output of the HJB computation is a stochastic viability map—a scalar field over the state space indicating the probability of remaining within safe physiological bounds under optimal control.

```
