Key feature: The boundary between viable and unviable regions is now gradual rather than sharp, reflecting the probabilistic nature of biological constraint satisfaction.

7.6 The Optimization Objective

The engineering goal shifts from point-optimization to maximizing the probability-weighted volume of the viability kernel:

\max_{U(\cdot)} \int_{\mathbb{R}^4} v(x, t; U) \, dx

This ensures maximum tolerance to unexpected environmental or pathogen perturbations while respecting the stochastic nature of biological systems.

7.7 Environment-Dependent Viability Maps

The viability map is environment-dependent:

· Earth Mode: v_{\text{Earth}}(x, t) — constraints defined by terrestrial disease prevention thresholds (ACSM, WHO, ISCD).
· Space Mode: v_{\text{Space}}(x, t) — constraints defined by mission readiness thresholds (which may be more stringent for some variables, e.g., higher minimum \dot{V}O_2\max for EVA capability).

The relationship between these maps—overlap, containment, disjointness—is an empirical question that the Layer B analysis can answer using bed-rest data as a Space-mode proxy.

