9. The Hybrid Physics-Informed Adaptive Predictive Model (Layer A)

9.1 From "Digital Twin" to "Adaptive Predictive Model"

True digital twins require sufficiently accurate generative models, continuous data streams, uncertainty quantification, and computational tractability. Human physiology is a partially observed, non-stationary, high-dimensional system. A full-body digital twin is not a near-term computational reality.

The Layer A architecture therefore adopts the language of Personalized Physiological State Estimator or Adaptive Predictive Model—more honest about achievable capabilities.

9.2 Hybrid Physics-Informed Neural Differential Equation (PINDE)

\frac{dX}{dt} = \underbrace{f_{\text{known}}(X, U, \theta_{\text{bio}})}_{\text{First-Principles Physiology}} + \underbrace{\text{NN}_{\phi}(X, U, E)}_{\text{Neural ODE Residual}}

where:

· f_{\text{known}}: Mass-balance equations for substrate utilization and lumped-parameter cardiovascular hemodynamics;
· \text{NN}_{\phi}: Deep neural network absorbing unmodeled individual heterogeneity and complex cross-domain feedback.

9.3 Training and Validation

The PINDE model is trained and validated using publicly available datasets (UK Biobank, All of Us, NASA GeneLab, Framingham). Validation protocols include cross-validation, prospective prediction, intervention simulation, and counterfactual analysis.

