10. Ethics, Data Governance, and Regulatory Compliance

10.1 Informed Consent and Data Privacy

A framework touching genomics, epigenetics, continuous biometric monitoring, and predictive modeling requires explicit informed consent for data collection, storage, and use. Where data are drawn from existing cohorts (UK Biobank, All of Us, NASA OSDR), the framework complies with each dataset's access protocols and data-use agreements.

For future prospective implementation:

· Genomic + wearable data linkage requires enhanced privacy protections (differential privacy, federated learning)
· Individuals retain data access, correction, and deletion rights
· Secondary research use requires explicit opt-in where feasible

10.2 Regulatory Pathway

If the HRSB moves toward clinical implementation:

· Software as Medical Device (SaMD): The Adaptive Predictive Model would require regulatory clearance (FDA 510(k) or De Novo pathway in the US; CE marking under MDR in the EU) before clinical deployment.
· Institutional Review Board (IRB): Any human subjects research involving intervention recommendations based on the model requires IRB approval.
· Clinical Decision Support: The framework positions itself as a decision-support tool, not an autonomous clinical decision-maker.

10.3 Autonomy and Medicalization

The framework is a tool, not an ideology. Individuals may legitimately choose not to optimize. The "space-ready human" is not a moral requirement, but an engineering specification. The philosophical stance: increase healthspan and functional capacity without unnecessary medicalization or infringement on individual freedom.

Note on the anti-perfectionist commitment: The stochastic viability framework is actually well-suited to this philosophical stance. Viability theory guarantees existence within constraints rather than optimization of performance—a formalization that respects individual freedom to choose suboptimal but acceptable health trajectories.

10.4 Governance Mechanisms

Evidence Classification Committee: Multidisciplinary panel (physiology, medicine, biostatistics, ethics) reviews evidence and assigns GRADE classifications.

Update Triggers: New randomized controlled trials, meta-analyses, adverse event reports, regulatory changes.

Conflict Resolution: Individual autonomy respected; the framework does not recommend GRADE D interventions outside registered clinical trials.

