15. Philosophical Principle

The ultimate objective of the framework is not human perfection. It is:

To increase the number of healthy, autonomous, cognitively capable, and physically resilient years of human life while preserving individual freedom and avoiding unnecessary medicalization.

The "space-ready human" is therefore not defined by extraordinary athletic performance. It is defined by reserve: reserve against disease, physical stress, metabolic instability, sleep disruption, psychological stress, environmental extremes, and physiological decline.

The central engineering question becomes:

\boxed{\text{How much biological reserve can safely be created, maintained, and restored throughout the human life course?}}

This is not a question that requires spaceflight to answer—but spaceflight may help us discover what the answer must be.

Note on the pathology of reserve: The framework acknowledges that physiological reserve is not purely beneficial. Cardiac hypertrophy from endurance training increases arrhythmia risk. Muscular strength beyond functional thresholds may confer no additional benefit and may increase injury risk. Bone density above a certain threshold does not proportionally reduce fracture risk. The stochastic viability framework accommodates this by allowing for interval constraints [x_{\min}, x_{\max}] rather than half-spaces [x \ge x_{\min}], and this extension will be explored in future work.

