Appendix A: Mathematical Proofs and Derivations

A.1 Proof of Bilinear System Stability Under Countermeasure Control

Theorem A.1 (Stabilizability of the Bilinear Deconditioning System). Consider the bilinear control-affine system:

\frac{dX}{dt} = \mathbf{A}_0 X + \sum_{j=1}^{m} u_j(t) \mathbf{A}_j X + \mathbf{C}E + \mathbf{w}(t)

where \mathbf{A}_0 is Hurwitz (all eigenvalues have negative real parts) and the intervention matrices \mathbf{A}_j have positive diagonal entries. Then there exists a constant control u^* \in \mathcal{U} such that the closed-loop system is exponentially stable with decay rate strictly slower than the uncontrolled system.

Proof. Let V(X) = X^T P X be a Lyapunov function candidate, where P = P^T \succ 0 is symmetric positive definite. The time derivative of V along trajectories of the uncontrolled system (u_j = 0) is:

\dot{V}(X) = X^T (P\mathbf{A}_0 + \mathbf{A}_0^T P) X

Since \mathbf{A}_0 is Hurwitz, there exists P \succ 0 such that:

P\mathbf{A}_0 + \mathbf{A}_0^T P = -\mathbf{Q}_0

for some \mathbf{Q}_0 \succ 0. This follows from the standard Lyapunov equation.

Now consider the controlled system with constant controls u_j(t) = u_j^* \ge 0. The Lyapunov derivative becomes:

\dot{V}(X) = X^T \left[ P\left(\mathbf{A}_0 + \sum_j u_j^* \mathbf{A}_j\right) + \left(\mathbf{A}_0 + \sum_j u_j^* \mathbf{A}_j\right)^T P \right] X

= -X^T \mathbf{Q}_0 X + \sum_j u_j^* X^T (P\mathbf{A}_j + \mathbf{A}_j^T P) X

Since \mathbf{A}_j has positive diagonal entries and the off-diagonal terms represent coupling, for sufficiently small u_j^*, the negative-definite term -\mathbf{Q}_0 dominates. Specifically, let:

\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{Q}_0) = \min_{\|X\|=1} X^T \mathbf{Q}_0 X > 0

and define:

M_j = \max_{\|X\|=1} |X^T (P\mathbf{A}_j + \mathbf{A}_j^T P) X|

Then for any u_j^* < \lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{Q}_0) / (m \cdot M_j), we have:

\dot{V}(X) \le -\left(\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{Q}_0) - \sum_j u_j^* M_j\right) \|X\|^2 < 0

which establishes exponential stability of the controlled system. \blacksquare

Corollary A.1.1. The decay rate of the controlled system is strictly slower than the uncontrolled system, in the sense that the dominant eigenvalue satisfies:

\max_i \text{Re}\left(\lambda_i\left(\mathbf{A}_0 + \sum_j u_j^* \mathbf{A}_j\right)\right) > \max_i \text{Re}\left(\lambda_i(\mathbf{A}_0)\right)

This follows from the monotonic dependence of the Lyapunov derivative on u_j^*. \blacksquare

---

A.2 Derivation of the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman Equation for Stochastic Viability

Theorem A.2 (HJB Equation for the Viability Value Function). Let v(x, t) be defined as:

v(x, t) = \sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \mathbb{E}\left[ \inf_{s \in [t, T]} \mathbf{1}_K(X(s)) \;\middle|\; X(t) = x \right]

where X(s) evolves according to the controlled stochastic differential equation:

dX = \left(\mathbf{A}_0 + \sum_j u_j \mathbf{A}_j\right) X \, dt + \mathbf{C}E \, dt + \mathbf{G} \, dW

Then v satisfies the following Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman partial differential equation:

-\frac{\partial v}{\partial t} - \max_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left\{ \left[\left(\mathbf{A}_0 + \sum_j u_j \mathbf{A}_j\right) X + \mathbf{C}E\right]^T \nabla_x v + \frac{1}{2} \text{Tr}\left(\mathbf{G}\mathbf{G}^T \nabla_x^2 v\right) \right\} = 0

with terminal condition v(x, T) = \mathbf{1}_K(x).

Proof. The proof follows from the Dynamic Programming Principle (DPP). For any h > 0:

v(x, t) = \sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{[t, t+h]}} \mathbb{E}\left[ \mathbf{1}_K(X(t+h)) \cdot v(X(t+h), t+h) \;\middle|\; X(t) = x \right]

where the indicator \mathbf{1}_K(X(t+h)) accounts for the possibility of leaving the safe set before time t+h.

Applying Itô's lemma to v(X(t+h), t+h):

v(X(t+h), t+h) = v(x, t) + \int_t^{t+h} \left[ \frac{\partial v}{\partial s} + \mathcal{L}_u v \right] ds + \int_t^{t+h} \nabla_x v^T \mathbf{G} \, dW

where \mathcal{L}_u is the infinitesimal generator of the controlled process:

\mathcal{L}_u v = \left[\left(\mathbf{A}_0 + \sum_j u_j \mathbf{A}_j\right) X + \mathbf{C}E\right]^T \nabla_x v + \frac{1}{2} \text{Tr}\left(\mathbf{G}\mathbf{G}^T \nabla_x^2 v\right)

Taking expectations and the limit h \to 0, we obtain:

0 = \frac{\partial v}{\partial t} + \sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \mathcal{L}_u v

Rearranging yields the HJB equation as stated. \blacksquare

Theorem A.3 (Viscosity Solution Existence and Uniqueness). Under the assumptions of Lipschitz continuity of the drift and diffusion coefficients and compactness of the control space \mathcal{U}, the HJB equation admits a unique continuous viscosity solution.

Proof. This follows from the general theory of viscosity solutions for second-order degenerate elliptic PDEs (Crandall, Ishii, and Lions, 1992). The verification is standard and omitted here; the key conditions are:

1. Boundedness of the Hamiltonian:

H(x, p, M) = \max_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left\{ \left[\left(\mathbf{A}_0 + \sum_j u_j \mathbf{A}_j\right) X + \mathbf{C}E\right]^T p + \frac{1}{2} \text{Tr}\left(\mathbf{G}\mathbf{G}^T M\right) \right\}

is continuous and satisfies the comparison principle.

2. Degenerate ellipticity: The matrix \mathbf{G}\mathbf{G}^T \succeq 0 is positive semidefinite.
3. Terminal condition regularity: The function \mathbf{1}_K(x) is lower semicontinuous, which is sufficient for the viscosity solution framework.

\blacksquare

---

A.3 Derivation of the Transferability Matrix Estimator

Definition A.3 (Directional Gradient of State Dynamics). For the bilinear system:

\dot{X} = \mathbf{A}_0 X + \sum_j u_j \mathbf{A}_j X + \mathbf{C}E

the gradient of the state derivative with respect to the control u_k is:

\nabla_{u_k} \dot{X} = \mathbf{A}_k X

Theorem A.4 (Cosine Similarity Estimator for Transferability). The Transferability Matrix entry T_{ij} is estimated as:

\hat{T}_{ij} = \frac{\hat{\mathbf{g}}_i^{(\text{Earth})} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{g}}_j^{(\text{Space})}}{\|\hat{\mathbf{g}}_i^{(\text{Earth})}\| \cdot \|\hat{\mathbf{g}}_j^{(\text{Space})}\|}

where the gradient vectors are estimated from fitted models:

\hat{\mathbf{g}}_i^{(\text{Earth})} = \frac{\partial \hat{\dot{x}}_i^{(\text{Earth})}}{\partial u}, \qquad
\hat{\mathbf{g}}_j^{(\text{Space})} = \frac{\partial \hat{\dot{x}}_j^{(\text{Space})}}{\partial u}

Proof of Consistency. As the number of observations N \to \infty, the estimated model parameters converge to their true values under standard regularity conditions:

\hat{\mathbf{A}}_k^{(mode)} \to \mathbf{A}_k^{(mode)} \quad \text{in probability}

Therefore:

\hat{\mathbf{g}}_i^{(mode)} = \hat{\mathbf{A}}_k^{(mode)} x_i \to \mathbf{A}_k^{(mode)} x_i = \mathbf{g}_i^{(mode)}

By the continuous mapping theorem, the cosine similarity estimator converges to the true cosine similarity. \blacksquare

Confidence Intervals. Bootstrap confidence intervals are constructed by resampling subjects with replacement, refitting the model, and recomputing \hat{T}_{ij}. The 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the bootstrap distribution provide a 95% confidence interval.

