A.4 Identifiability Analysis of the Bilinear Model

Theorem A.5 (Structural Identifiability of the Bilinear Control System). The bilinear system with observation matrix \mathbf{H}(t) is structurally identifiable if and only if:

\text{rank}\left( \begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{H} \\
\mathbf{H}\mathbf{A}_0 \\
\mathbf{H}\mathbf{A}_j \\
\mathbf{H}\mathbf{A}_0\mathbf{A}_j \\
\vdots
\end{bmatrix} \right) = 4

for all j = 1, \ldots, m, where the matrices are stacked vertically.

Proof. Structural identifiability requires that the mapping from parameters \{\mathbf{A}_0, \mathbf{A}_j\} to the output sequence \{Y_k\} is injective. For linear time-invariant systems with bilinear control, the observability matrix with control-dependent terms must have full rank.

The augmented observability matrix includes all products of system matrices with intervention matrices, since the bilinear structure generates terms of the form \mathbf{H}\mathbf{A}_0\mathbf{A}_j X in the output derivatives. \blacksquare

Practical Identifiability Assessment. Structural identifiability is necessary but not sufficient. Practical identifiability is assessed via:

1. Fisher Information Matrix (FIM):

\text{FIM}(\Theta) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \left( \frac{\partial \hat{Y}_k}{\partial \Theta} \right)^T \mathbf{R}_k^{-1} \left( \frac{\partial \hat{Y}_k}{\partial \Theta} \right)

2. Parameter Correlations:

\rho_{ij} = \frac{[\text{FIM}^{-1}]_{ij}}{\sqrt{[\text{FIM}^{-1}]_{ii} [\text{FIM}^{-1}]_{jj}}}

Parameters with |\rho_{ij}| > 0.95 are considered practically unidentifiable as a pair.

3. Monte Carlo Estimation Error: As described in Section 6.6, the relative estimation error across simulated datasets provides an empirical measure of practical identifiability.

